# Untitled

## Metal-organic frameworks as photocatalysts for aerobic oxidation reactions
College of Chemistry and Materials Science, Guangdong Provincial Key Laboratory of Functional Supramolecular Coordination Materials and Applications, Jinan University, Guangzhou 510632, China
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are emerging as one of the most intriguing classes of heterogeneous photocatalysts owing to their abundant structures, tunable porosity, and versatile functions. The advantages of bottom-up design and reticular synthesis render MOF materials with desired photocatalytic properties for targeted reactions. In this review, we discussed the design and synthesis of MOF-based photocatalysts as well as strategies for enhancing photocatalytic performance. Recent progress on MOFs as platforms for photocatalytic aerobic oxidation reactions was summarized and categorized according to the types of bond formation. We hope this review will give an in-depth insight into MOF-based photocatalytic systems for not only aerobic oxidation reactions but also other organic transformations. A brief outlook on the challenges and opportunities of MOFs as heterogeneous photocatalysts is provided at the end of the review.
metal-organic frameworks, heterogeneous photocatalyst, reactive oxygen species, aerobic oxidation, organic synthesis
Citation: Wu K, Liu XY, Cheng PW, Xie M, Lu W, Li D. Metal-organic frameworks as photocatalysts for aerobic oxidation reactions. Sci China Chem, 2023, 66: 1634–1653, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-022-1519-x
## 1 Introduction
Global energy crisis and environmental issues have been becoming increasingly prominent with the continuous growth of the world economy [1–3]. Traditional energy resources such as coal, oil, and natural gas not only face imminent exhaustion but also fail to meet the modern social requirement of green and sustainability. On the other hand, solar energy is clean, renewable, and inexhaustible [4–6]. Converting solar energy into electrical or chemical energy is expected to be one of the effective approaches to address the global energy crisis. Inspired by the photosynthesis in nature, researchers in the chemical industry have put a great deal of effort into artificial photosynthesis, converting carbon dioxide into transportable energy-rich molecules (e.g., methane, carbon monoxide, formic acid, formaldehyde, methanol) and high-value-added organic products [7,8]. The so-called "photocatalysis" could be an ideal clean-energy approach and provide green alternatives to traditional chemical reactions and industrial production. Meanwhile, the utilization of light energy may have unique effects on chemical reactions, such as changing their thermodynamic equilibria and enabling the reactions to proceed under mild conditions. Therefore, photocatalytic reactions could potentially avoid side reactions that occur under traditional thermal conditions and achieve higher reaction selectivity.
