In the past several decades, photocatalysis has been one of the hottest research topics in the fields of chemistry, materials, and engineering [9–11]. Great progress has been made in the development of heterogeneous photocatalysts since the discovery of electrochemical photolysis of water over \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) semiconductor by Fujishima and Honda [12]. Other semiconductors (ZnO, \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\), etc.) were subsequently found to
also possess the photocatalytic activity and were gradually widely used in photocatalysis because of their low toxicity, low cost, and high stability. A photocatalytic process can be broken down into light absorption, generation of charge carriers (electron-hole pairs), and transfer of charge carriers to initiate redox reactions [13–16]. Each of these steps will have huge impact on the overall photocatalytic performance. A narrow band gap may promote the photogeneration of electrons and holes due to the enhanced light absorption in the low-energy region. Yet, it could also facilitate the recombination of these electrons and holes. In most instances, the photocatalytic efficiency is hinged upon the competition of charge separation and recombination. Therefore, it is of great significance to explore photocatalysts by rationally adjusting their compositions and structures, aiming for suitable band gaps and improved charge separation.
As an emerging class of porous crystalline materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are constructed with metal ions/clusters and organic linkers and are known for their exceptional porosity, functional modifiability, and structural flexibility. They show great potential in photocatalysis and may provide a solution to the issues associated with traditional semiconductor-based photocatalysts and inorganic porous materials, such as low light harvesting efficiency, difficulty in regulating electron-hole separation, overly concentrated active sites, and poor reaction selectivity (Figure 1). There are several advantages of MOFs being heterogeneous photocatalysts: (1) MOFs are crystalline frameworks with precise locations of the photocatalytically active sites; (2) the modular construction of MOFs allows for easy incorporation of photocatalytically active motifs; (3) light absorption properties and charge separation efficiency are largely tunable in MOFs benefitting from their abundant and diverse structures; (4) the porous nature endows MOFs with more exposed active sites and fast transportation of reactants and products, which is conducive to the transfer of the photogenerated charge carriers and thus avoiding the rapid recombination of electrons and holes; (5) MOFs are excellent platforms to construct composite materials by incorporating functional guest molecules into the pores and the photocatalytic performance can be thus finely tuned [17–25]. The modifiability and porosity of MOFs is one of the outstanding advantages over traditional semiconductor photocatalysts. Compared with other porous materials (such as porous organic polymers, POPs; covalent organic frameworks, COFs), the ordered structures of MOFs and precise location of active sites can be conveniently utilized for exploring the structure-activity relationship and understanding the catalytic mechanism. Although seemingly endless MOF structures are already available, great efforts are still required for the judicious design and synthesis of new MOF materials as photocatalysts. On the other hand, modification and regulation of the known MOF photocatalysts are also necessary
to further improve the catalytic performance and selectivity. Diatomic oxygen \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\) is an ideal oxidant for organic transformations and has gained much attention in recent years owing to its availability and environmental friendliness. Visible-light-driven \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) conversion into reactive oxygen species (ROS) has been frequently used for different aerobic oxidation reactions. To integrate existing experience and guide new researchers on this topic, a summary and generalization of the current MOF-based photocatalysts for aerobic oxidation reactions is needed. Thus, we surveyed the recent scientific literature on MOF-based photocatalysts and their application in the photocatalysis of aerobic oxidation reactions. In this review, we first briefly introduced the generation and detection of ROS. According to the proposed ROS generation mechanisms, the design, synthesis, and property regulation of MOF-based photocatalysts were summarized, and the strategies for improving the photocatalytic performance were discussed. With that, the aerobic oxidation reactions catalyzed by MOF-based platforms were highlighted and sorted out according to their formed chemical bonds. The history of MOF-based heterogeneous photocatalysts is relatively short, but a large number of interesting studies have already emerged, emphasizing the potential of MOF materials in heterogeneous photocatalysis. At the end of this review, we give a brief outlook on the future development of MOF-based heterogeneous photocatalysts in aerobic oxidation reactions.
## 2 Generation and detection of reactive oxygen species (ROS)
In aerobic oxidation reactions with MOFs as photocatalysts, the ROS generation processes are generally the same as the ones with traditional heterogeneous photocatalysts. As depicted in Figure 1, the catalytic processes include light absorption of photocatalysts, generation of charge carriers (electrons and holes), recombination or transfer of photogenerated charge carriers to generate ROS, and ROS participation in redox reactions. The electrons with high
reduction potential were photoexcited from the valence band (VB) to the conduction band (CB), while the holes with high oxidation power occupy the VB. In some cases, this electron-hole separation process has also been described with molecular orbital (MO) theory, with the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) accommodating the photogenerated electrons, while the photogenerated holes reside in the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO). In this review, band theory was applied to describe this electron-hole separation process.
In photocatalytic aerobic oxidation reactions, the photogenerated electrons and holes are not directly involved in the organic transformations. Instead, they initiate the conversion of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) into ROS. There are four major ROS that have been recognized and well investigated: singlet oxygen \(\left({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\), superoxide anion radical \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\right)\), hydroxyl radical \(\left(\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet}\right)\), and hydrogen peroxide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\). \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) at triplet ground state can absorb the radiation energy from charge recombination, generating the highly electrophilic \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). This process was generally summarized as energy transfer (ET), in which MOFs act as either photosensitizers or energy-transfer donors, supplying energy for triplet to singlet \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) transition. On the other hand, the photogenerated electron can be directly transferred to ground state \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), generating the highly nucleophilic \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\). This process was generally summarized as single electron transfer (SET). The \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) can be further transformed into \(\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet}\) in the presence of protons through a series of reactions. And the photogenerated holes can directly oxidize \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) into \(\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Interestingly, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) can also be formed by the dimerization of \(\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet}\), while Fenton's reagents are frequently utilized to convert \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet}\left(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}+\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet}\right)\). It should be pointed out that \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) and \(\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet}\) are sometimes generated simultaneously in photocatalytic reactions, which may greatly affect the reaction selectivity. Strategies such as band gap engineering and surface treatment were usually employed to avoid the coexistence of \(\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\).
Precisely controlled ROS generation and their efficient utilization in organic reactions are at the core of photocatalysis. Thus, the characterization of ROS, including identification, quantification, and reaction kinetics, is of paramount importance in understanding the reaction mechanism. Such information on ROS and how they are involved in catalytic cycles can be fed back to the regulation of the structures and properties of the MOFs photocatalysts, ultimately achieving the goal of improving solar energy conversion efficiency. At present, the detection of ROS relies primarily on electron spin resonance (ESR) techniques, also known as electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR). The free states of most ROS in photocatalytic reactions cannot be observed directly by ESR due to the limitations of spintronic degeneracy, weak signal strength, short lifetime, and gas phase detection conditions. Utilizing additional reagents as
spin trapping or probe reagents has become an indirect but effective approach for the ESR detection of ROS. In particular, 4-oxo-2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine (TEMP) can be converted into tetramethylpiperidine-N-oxyl (TEMPO) in \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) environment and the latter displays intensive characteristic signals in ESR, therefore it can be employed for the detection of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [26–28]. 5,5-Dimethyl-1-pyrroline-N-oxide (DMPO) is the commonly used radical trapping reagent for \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) and \(\mathrm{OH}^{\bullet}\), the formed radical adducts have long lifetimes and therefore can be easily recognized by ESR [29–33]. The addition of scavengers is also a simple and feasible method for the detection of ROS [34,35]. For example, 1,5-dihydroxynaphthalene (DHN), 1,3-diphenylisobenzofuran (DPBF), and 9,10-diphenylanthracene (DPA) can be used as indirect calibration reagents for the amount of the photogenerated \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Similarly, the amount of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) can be quantified with nitro blue tetrazolium chloride monohydrate (NBT) (Figure 2). Besides the above-mentioned detection methods, there are other reports on ROS detection, including direct light emission by \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [36–38], optical absorption of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [39–42], color reaction, and fluorescent products detection [42–45].
## 3 Construction of MOF-based photocatalysts
An efficient heterogeneous photocatalytic system should have wide-range light absorption, fast charge transfer, and high affinity for substrates while low affinity for products. MOF materials can integrate the required functions for photocatalysis by introducing photocatalytic motifs at the construction stage or forming photocatalytic complexes afterwards. In this section, several strategies for the construction of MOF-based photocatalysts were exemplified, emphasizing on direct employment of photocatalytic build
ing blocks, including metal nodes and organic ligands, and post-synthetic modification (PSM), including ligand metalation and encapsulation of photoactive species.
## 3.1 Metal clusters as photocatalytic centers
In 1999, Yaghi's group [46] reported the synthesis and gas adsorption of a three-dimensional (3D) MOF (MOF-5), a milestone in the history of MOF development. Since then, numerous MOFs with high specific surface area have been reported, showing great potential in various applications, especially in the field of catalysis. An obvious approach for utilizing MOFs as photocatalysts is to make organic bridging ligands light-absorbing antennas, which in turn transfer energy and activate metal clusters to initiate the reactions. In 2004, Zecchina's group [47] investigated the light absorption and photoluminescence properties of MOF-5. Ultraviolet-visible diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (UV-vis-DRS) and photoluminescence spectroscopy (PL) showed that the light absorption range and luminescence performance of MOF-5 were better than \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) and disodium terephthalate, which were attributed to the ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) in MOF-5. The results indicated that the \( \mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{13} \) clusters in MOF-5 may function as \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) quantum dots (QDs), enabling MOF-5 as a promising photocatalyst (Figure 3). In 2007, Garcia's group [48] provided an important piece of evidence that MOF-5 was indeed a semiconductor material. The laser flash spectroscopy of MOF-5 suggested the existence of a transient species, which the authors attributed to the ligand donating electrons to the \( \mathrm{Zn} \) cluster under light irradiation. The presence of the transient species in this charge-separated state is on the order of microseconds. In 2008, Gascon's group [49] reported the synthesis of a series of isostructural MOFs constructed from \( \mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2} \cdot 6 \) \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) and linear dicarboxylic acids with different functional groups. The obtained MOFs were used to explore the photocatalytic propylene oxidation reactions. Among them, 2,6-naphtha-MOF has a band gap similar to \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) and \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \), yet its catalytic efficiency is higher than \( \mathrm{ZnO} \). This is the first example of MOFs as photocatalyst for organic transformation.
Inspired by the wide application of \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) in photocatalysis, Férey's group [50] designed and synthesized a Ti-MOF (MIL-125-Ti) with Ti-O clusters and terephthalate ligands, then adopted it as photocatalyst for the oxidation of alcohols. Under the reaction conditions, the color of MIL-125-Ti changed from white to purple to gray to blue. ESR studies confirmed the interconversion from Ti(III) to Ti(IV), implying that the Ti-O clusters act as active centers during the oxidation process. Unfortunately, MIL-125-Ti can only be excited by high-energy UV light, which limits the practical application of such material. Since the emerging of the first Zr-MOF (UiO-66) in 2008, a growing number of researchers have devoted themselves to developing Zr-based MOFs as
photocatalysts because they are relatively easy to synthesize [51]. Different from Ti-O clusters of MIL-125-Ti and Zr-O clusters of UiO-66, Fe-O clusters can be directly excited by visible light [52]. For example, Li's group [53] employed MIL-100(Fe) or MIL-68(Fe) as photocatalyst and \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) as oxidant for the conversion of benzene to phenol under visible light irradiation, a Fenton-like oxidation reaction (Figure 4). It is worth noting that the only by-product of this reaction is \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \), reflecting the green chemistry characteristics of heterogeneous photocatalysis.
Recently, Potter's group [54] reported the synthesis of two Co-based zeolitic imidazole frameworks (ZIF-9-Co and ZIF-67-Co) by using benzimidazole and 2-methylimidazole, respectively. Both of them showed broad visible-light absorption. Interestingly, the photocatalytic performance of ZIF-9-Co towards the aza-henry cross-dehydrogenative coupling (CDC) reaction is better than that of ZIF-67-Co under visible light irradiation. The UV-vis-DRS and in situ X-ray adsorption spectroscopy (XAS) showed that the LMCT of ZIF-67-Co is stronger than that of ZIF-9-Co. LMCT can enhance the electron density of Co atoms, reducing their interaction with substrates in the system. On the other hand, the increase of electron density generally makes the Co atoms prone to oxidation, yet the reduction of photocatalyst promotes the CDC reaction; therefore, the increase of LMCT in the CDC reaction will inhibit the photocatalytic efficiency. Notably, due to the restricted transport of substrates within the small pores of ZIF-9-Co, the authors speculated that the catalysis may occur on the surface of MOFs. Moreover, the reusability test showed that the MOFs collected by filtration can be reused at least four times without showing an obvious loss of catalytic property.
## 3.2 Organic ligands as photocatalytic centers
Although there have been some successful examples, using metal clusters as photocatalytic centers in MOFs is quite limited due to their weak and narrow visible light absorption. Therefore, it is a common practice to introduce photoactive organic ligands, such as porphyrin and its derivatives, and pyrene and its derivatives, in the construction of MOF materials for photocatalysis.
Directly using photosensitive bridging ligands is one of the general approaches to constructing photocatalytically active MOFs. Perylene imides (PDIs), a class of organic dye molecules with high photochemical activity, have attracted extensive attention due to their photoinduced electron transfer (PET) process, overcoming the energetic limitations of visible light photocatalysis and promoting the photocatalytic conversion of less reactive chemical bonds (e.g., aryl bromides and chlorides) in organic transformation [55]. In 2016, Duan's group [56] synthesized a Zn-based MOF (Zn-PDI) by using a PDI dicarboxylate as ligand. Under visible light irradiation, Zn-PDI can function as a heterogeneous photocatalyst and realize various types of photocatalytic organic reactions, such as reduction of halogenated hydrocarbons, coupling of aryl halides and \(N\!\cdot\) -methylpyrroles, and oxidation of benzyl alcohols (Figure 5). More importantly, Zn-PDI exhibits much improved photocatalytic activity compared with PDI ligand itself. This phenomenon was attributed to the ordered arrangement of PDI ligands in Zn-PDI framework, which can stabilize the radical-anion intermediate and facilitate catalyst-substrate interaction. The synergistic effect between \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking of PDI molecules and metal-PDI coordination can further facilitate the oxidation of benzyl alcohols and amines.
The light absorption, oxygen transport, and catalytic property of metalloporphyrins are one of the cornerstones of life on earth. Porphyrin molecules are also known for activating molecular \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and generating \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) under
visible light irradiation [57–60]. In recent years, porphyrin derivatives have been frequently used as organic bridging ligands to construct MOFs for artificial photosynthesis and photocatalysis [61]. In 2014, Zhang's group [62] reported a series of In-based MOFs self-assembled with different metalized tetra-3,5-dibenzoylbenzene-substituted porphyrins and applied them as photocatalysts for the oxidation of sulfur ethers. It was found that the change of metal/ligand ratios during the synthesis and the metallization of In(III) can finetune the charge density of the MOFs and further control the performance of the selective oxidation of sulfides. Farha's group [63] applied a porphyrin-based MOF (PCN-222) as photocatalyst for the oxidation of a toxic mustard gas simulant (2-chlorodiethyl sulfide, CEES) into a non-toxic sulfoxide product (Figure 6). Interestingly, PCN-222 showed excellent selectivity and no further oxidation of the sulfoxide into sulfone was observed. Meanwhile, Zhou's group [64] designed and synthesized a Ti-based MOF (PCN-22) with tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP) ligand. After \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) of visible light \((>\!450\;\mathrm{nm})\) irradiation and in the presence of PCN-22, benzyl alcohols were selectively converted into the corresponding benzaldehydes with up to \(100\%\) yield, and its performance is better than that of the \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -based MOF with the same ligand (PCN-224), reflecting the photoactivity of Ti-O clusters. In 2021, our group [60] reported a series of Labased MOFs (M-TCPP-La, M-TCPP \(=\) metal-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin) as heterogeneous photocatalysts for the selective oxidation of CEES. This work demonstrated that \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) can be both used for the detoxification of mustard gas for the first time. The photocatalytic performance of Fe-TCPP-La was found to be better than those of the other M-TCPP-La \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}\) , Ni, \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) , and Mn), which is consistent with their ROS generation capability.
Pyrene is a widely used organic chromophore whose
photophysical properties have been extensively studied [65–67]. In 2016, Farha's group [68] employed a pyrene-based MOF (NU-1000) as heterogeneous photocatalyst for the selective oxidation of CEES into the non-toxic product 2-chloroethylethyl sulfoxide (CEESO) under ultraviolet light-emitting diode (UV-LED) irradiation. Its excellent photocatalytic properties were attributed to the efficient singlet oxygen yielding throughout the energy transfer from the excited triplet photosensitizer to triplet oxygen at the ground state. In 2019, Zhou's group [69] constructed two porous \(Z\mathbf{r}/\) Hf-MOFs using a tetratopic pyrene-based ligand with light absorption in the range of \(225{-}650\ \mathrm{nm}\) . Under visible light irradiation, the catalytic oxidation of benzylamine can be achieved under mild conditions without other chromophores. In terms of photooxidation of DHN, the catalytic activity of Zr/Hf-MOFs is significantly improved compared with the corresponding organic ligand, and the catalytic efficiency of Hf-MOF is better than that of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOF. Besides the wide light absorption range of pyrene molecules, the excellent photocatalytic performance of Hf-MOF was attributed to the heavy metal effect of hafnium for the enhanced intersystem crossing, which promotes the generation of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) .
Anthracene and its derivatives are also a class of chromophores [70,71]. In 2019, Xing's group [72] used anthracene-based derivatives to construct a novel 2-fold interpenetrated In-MOF with good chemical stability and broad visible light absorption. Under the visible light irradiation, \({{\mathrm{O}}_{2}}^{\bullet-}\) was generated in the presence of the In-MOF and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , initiating the photooxidation of benzylamine and thioether compounds. Anthraquinone and its derivatives have been widely used as photocatalysts in many chemical transformations due to the high energy and long lifetime of
their triplet states. Furthermore, the inert \(\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{sp}^{3}){-}\mathrm{H}\) bond can be activated and oxidized through a typical light-driven hydrogen atom transfer (HAT) process [73–75]. Recently, Duan's group [76] demonstrated a photocatalytic platform for the synergistic effect of HAT and ROS generation by rationally introducing anthraquinones into MOFs. The anthraquinones are immobilized on the channel walls inside the MOFs, which not only increases the interaction between the catalytic sites and the organic substrates through \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions, but also facilitates the formation and stabilization of the free radicals through adjacent anthraquinones. Furthermore, the uniformly distributed anthraquinones are conducive to \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) activation and generating \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) through energy transfer, enabling green catalysis of a series of substrates containing benzyl \(\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{sp}^{3}){-}\mathrm{H}\) bonds under visible light irradiation.
The poor solubility of some organic photosensitizers may limit the crystallinity of the obtained MOFs, which could hinder further studies of the structure and property relationship. Therefore, a mixed ligand strategy is usually used to construct MOFs with both photoactivity and crystallinity. For example, Lin's group successfully synthesized six mixed-ligand \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs (UiO-67 type) by mixing Ir, Re, and Ru-based dicarboxylic acid complexes with bpdc ( \(\boldsymbol{p}\) -biphenyldicarboxylate) linker, respectively. The obtained Zr-MOFs were used for photocatalytic water oxidation, photochemical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, and photocatalytic organic reaction [77]. Liu's group [78] also constructed a novel MOF by using a mixed-ligand strategy with a PDI derivative and naphthalenedicarboxylate as ligands, in which PDI is the photoactive unit. Under mild conditions, the obtained MOF was demonstrated as an efficient heterogeneous photocatalyst for the perfluorooidoalkylation of alkenes and the oxidation of benzylamines.
Benzothiazole and benzoselenadiazole are organic heterocyclic molecules with excellent photoluminescence properties and photocatalytic activity [4,79–86]. Their derivatives can also be used as ligands to construct MOFs and achieve ROS generation under visible light irradiation, thereby realizing photocatalytic oxidation reactions. In 2016, Wang's group adopted a mixed-ligand strategy to incorporate a benzoselenadiazole-containing ligand into UiO-68, and the obtained UiO-68-Se was used in the dehydrogenation coupling reaction of tetrahydroisoquinoline (THQ) [87]. In 2017, Farha's group [88] applied a mixed-ligand strategy and constructed PCN-57-Se and PCN-57-S with benzoselenadiazole/benzothiadiazole and \(2^{\prime}.3^{\prime}.5^{\prime}.6^{\prime}\) -tetramethylterphenyl- \(\cdot4,4^{\prime\prime}\) -dicarboxylate, respectively, achieving selective photocatalytic oxidation of mustard gas simulant. By combining steady-state emission and time-resolved emission spectroscopic analysis with theoretical calculations, it was found that the selenodiazole-containing MOF had more efficient excited state singlet to triplet intersystem crossing,
confirming the relatively high catalytic activity of PCN-57-Se. Wang's group introduced a photoactive triazolethiazole (TBTD)-conjugated triphenyldicarboxylate (TPDC) linker into UiO-68 using a mixed-ligand strategy [89]. Under blue LED irradiation, the obtained UiO-68-TBTD exhibits a better catalytic effect on sulfide oxidation than PCN-57-Se. These results demonstrate that the introduction of conjugated groups can further improve the photocatalytic property.
catalytic performance in visible-light-driven CDC reaction and outperformed the homogeneous \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]\) catalyst. The highly efficient photocatalytic activity of FJI-Y2 was attributed to the intermolecular interaction between \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]\) and NDI radicals, which accelerates the PET process and the turnover of the photocatalyst. In 2019, Zhang's group [19] demonstrated a template-directed synthesis method for preparing heterogeneous photocatalysts with homogeneous photocatalysts as structure-directing agents (Figure 7). A series of porous zeolite-like MOFs with sodium salt-type cages can be obtained when \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]\) or \(\left[\mathrm{Ir}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]\) photoactive species were used as templates. The \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\)-encapsulated MOF \(\left(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}@\mathrm{NKMOF}-4\right)\) showed better catalytic activity and good reusability for the oxidation of halogenated benzyl oxide and the cyclization of tertiary aniline with maleimide under visible light irradiation. The authors attributed it to the good dispersion of photocatalysts inside the MOFs and the efficient transport of reactants and products. In addition, the authors suggest that the template-guided strategy to convert homogeneous catalysts into heterogeneous systems can be extended to other MOF systems. Around the same time, Cao and co-workers [101] reported the encapsulation of \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) into the pores of MIL-125-Ti using a similar method. The experimental results confirm the efficient electron transfer between \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) and MIL-125-Ti under visible light irradiation, which improves the separation of photogenerated charge carriers.
Polyoxometalates (POMs) are a class of discrete anionic metal oxide clusters with fast, reversible, and stepwise multielectron transfer properties. Therefore, they usually show excellent photocatalytic activity and are often used to enhance the separation process of electron-hole pairs in photocatalytic reactions [102,103]. In 2013, Zheng's group [104] prepared nanocrystalline MOF composites of different sizes by encapsulating \(\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{PV}_{2}\mathrm{Mo}_{10}\mathrm{O}_{40}\) (POM) into the pores of Cu-based MOF (MENU-9N) through both solution and mechanical grinding methods. It was found that the catalytic effect of these MOF composites in the oxidative desulfurization reaction was enhanced with the decrease of the particle size, which was attributed to the shortened diffusion path for substrates and products. In 2015, Lin's group [105] encapsulated noble-metal-free POMs into UiO-series MOFs constructed with \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]\)-containing ligand using a one-pot synthesis method. Under visible light irradiation, electrons can be rapidly transferred from the \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]\) unit to the POM, realizing the highly efficient photocatalytic activity. Recently, Wang's group [106] investigated two POM@MOFs (PMo-1 and SiW-2). The photocatalytic studies demonstrate that the combination of \(\left[\mathrm{Ru}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]\) and POM not only enhances the visible light absorption but also improves the charge carrier separation and transfer efficiency. For example, PMo-1 was used as an efficient heterogeneous photocatalyst for the oxidative coupling of
benzylamines under mild conditions with good recyclability and reusability.
Metal nanoparticles (NPs) can also be immobilized in the pores of MOFs as guests to facilitate the charge separation and therefore improve the catalytic activity of the MOF composites [107]. Li and colleagues [108] reported the synthesis of a Pd NPs encapsulation method, in which Pd nanocrystals precursors and \(\mathrm{MIL}-125-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) were added to polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) solutions, and the further addition of ascorbic acid as reducing agent resulted in a series of \(\mathrm{MIL}-125-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) with ultrafine Pd NPs encapsulated. The optimal loading of Pd NPs was established by investigating the catalytic efficiency in the benzylamine oxidation reaction. The substrate scope was expanded and the catalyst showed good recyclability. PL spectroscopy, photoelectrochemical measurements, and tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) oxidation experiments all confirmed that the introduction of ultrafine Pd NPs plays an important role in promoting charge generation and transfer.
Charge transfer between metal NPs and the supporting matrix is vital for photocatalysis. In previous reports, it has been concluded that redox-inert supports are generally disadvantageous due to less efficient electron interactions with metal NPs. In 2022, Jiang's group [109] selected both redox-inert (ZIF-8) and -active MOFs (UiO-66 and MIL-125) for encapsulating metal NPs, and their photocatalytic properties were investigated. The experimental results suggest, however, that the above conclusions are not necessarily correct. The Schottky junction formed between Pt NPs and MOFs
renders Pt/ZIF-8 a good electron donor with higher electron density compared with Pt/UiO-66 and Pt/MIL-125. Under visible light irradiation, both the electron-donation effect of MOF and interband excitation of Pt NPs contribute to the effective generation of photo-electron, resulting in superior photocatalytic activity of Pt/ZIF-8 for the oxidation of benzylamine.
Encapsulating quantum dots (QDs) in MOFs is also a common applied strategy to improve light absorption and enhance photocatalytic performance. CdS nanoparticles are QDs with strong luminescence [110]. In 2019, Zhang's group [111] synthesized a MIL-101 composite loaded with CdS QDs, showing much enhanced visible light absorption. Importantly, a strong interaction between the CdS nanoparticles and the MIL-101 matrix was confirmed by Raman spectroscopy. Transmission electron microscope (TEM) also showed a uniform distribution of CdS QDs. The CdS@MIL-101 composites demonstrated excellent photocatalytic performance in the oxidation of benzylamine and the oxidative coupling of benzylamine derivatives. In another work, Hou and co-workers [112] used the same method to load CdS into three Zr-MOFs, MOF-808, NU-1000, and PCN-222, respectively. The light-harvesting ability and charge transfer/separation efficiency of \(\mathbf{CdS}@\mathbf{Zr}\) -MOFs were confirmed by UV-vis-DRS and electrochemical measurements. Compared with the pristine Zr-MOFs, \(\mathbf{CdS}@\mathbf{Zr}\) - MOFs can significantly improve the catalytic activity for the oxidative coupling of benzylamine under air and visible light conditions. The authors attributed the efficient catalytic performance of \(\mathbf{CdS}@\mathbf{Zr}\) -MOFs to their significantly improved visible light absorption, more exposed active sites, and higher redox potential.
Fullerene \(\left(\mathbf{C}_{60}\right)\) consists of 30 bonded molecular orbitals and \(60\,\pi\) -electrons in its conjugated structure, rendering it an efficient generator of ROS [113,114]. In 2019, Huang's group [115] reported a \(\mathbf{C}_{60}@\mathbf{PCN-222}\) heterogeneous photocatalyst, in which \(\mathrm{{C}_{60}}\) molecules were encapsulated in PCN-222 as guest molecules. Compared with pristine PCN222, the photogenerated electron-hole separation of \(\mathbf{C}_{60}@\mathbf{PCN-222}\) is significantly enhanced. Under visible light irradiation, the conversion of thioether to sulfoxide was demonstrated with good selectivity and high yield. Furthermore, no significant loss of catalytic activity was observed during the catalyst reuse.
Recently, our group [116,117] reported an EY-encapsulated covalent-metal-organic framework (CMOF) composite \((\mathbf{EY}@\mathbf{JNM-1})\) , in which \({\mathrm{Cu(I)}}\) cyclic trinuclear units (CTUs) were used as nodes for framework construction. Compared with the pristine CMOF (JNM-1), \(\mathbf{EY}@\mathbf{JNM-1}\) exhibits wider visible light absorption, narrower band gap, and faster charge transfer. Under blue light irradiation, \(\mathbf{EY}@\mathbf{JNM-1}\) was demonstrated photocatalytic activity for the classical Sonogashira cross-coupling reac
tions without the addition of Pd salts, and the target molecules can be obtained at room temperature with high selectivity. Moreover, \(\mathbf{EY}@\mathbf{JNM-1}\) exhibits excellent stability and recyclability, which provides a feasible strategy for developing high-performance heterogeneous catalysts for Sonogashira cross-coupling reactions.
In addition to the above-mentioned encapsulation/deposition methods, post-modification method is also a common strategy for bringing photoactive species into the porous MOF materials. Farha's group [118] applied a solvent-assisted post-synthesis protocol and incorporated boron-di-pyrromethene (BODIYP) derivatives (Br-BDP) into a stable MOF (NU-1000). Compared with the unmodified NU-1000, Br-BDP-functionalized NU-1000 exhibited higher \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation efficiency and maintained structural integrity after catalyzing the oxidation of CEES. This work demonstrated a facile method to immobile the BODIPY molecules in MOFs to achieve rapid and selective oxidation of mustard gas mimics under visible light conditions.
## 4 Strategies for improving photocatalytic performance
At present, a fundamental principle for the design of photocatalysts is to expand the light absorption range and therefore improve the utilization of light energy. The electrons and holes generated from the excitation of photocatalysts are of high oxidation and reduction potency, dictating the ROS generation efficiency. Efficient separation of electrons and holes and preventing electrons and holes recombination on the catalyst surface while maintaining their high oxidation and reduction potency is another important principle for photocatalyst design. In this section, we summarized the strategies for improving the photocatalytic performance of MOF materials, in efforts to tune the band gap as well as promote charge separation and inhibit charge recombination.
## 4.1 Tuning the band gap
The commonly used strategy to broaden the light adsorption range of traditional semiconductor photocatalysts is modulating the band structures. As for MOF systems, band gap modulations are usually achieved through bottom-up structure regulation.
MOFs containing the oxophilic Ti-O clusters have been widely recognized in the field of photocatalysis due to their low toxicity and high redox capacity. In 2016, Yaghi and coworkers [119] reported an imine formation reaction of the in situ generated Ti-O clusters and terephthalaldehydes, affording a visible-light responsive Ti-MOF (MOF-901). Next, they synthesized an isostructural Ti-MOF (MOF-902)
by increasing the conjugation of the aldehyde [120]. Both TiMOFs were used in the photocatalytic polymerization of methyl methacrylate (MMA), the results suggest that the employment of \(4,4^{\prime}\)-biphenyldicarboxaldehyde enhance the visible-light absorption due to the increased conjugation, which further lower the VB level, reduce the band gap, and improve the catalytic efficiency of the polymerization.
Pyrene is a widely used organic chromophore whose unique optical and electronic properties have been extensively investigated [65–67]. In 2022, Farha's group [121] adopted a PSM strategy to introduce Ti species into a pyrene-based ZrMOF (NU-1000) (Figure 8). Interestingly, ligand migration was observed in the process, and the obtained NU-1012 exhibits a broadened visible-light absorption range and significantly enhanced ROS generation efficiency. The authors attributed this phenomenon to the dual active centers (pyrene-based ligands and \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{Ti}_{4}\)-based nodes) of NU-1012. The introduction of the Ti endows NU-1012 with a faster charge separation, lowered VB level, suppressed electron-hole pair recombination, higher ROS generation efficiency, and improved photocatalytic activity. The authors employed multiple characterization techniques to prove the ligand migration and cluster transformation from \(\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(OH)_{8}-(H_{2}O)_{4}]}\) to \(\mathrm{[Zr_{6}Ti_{4}O_{8}(OH)_{8}(H_{2}O)_{8}]}\), suggesting the generality of this method on Zr-MOFs with cqs topology [122].
In 2012, Liu and co-workers [123] experimentally demonstrated that the band gap width of Zn-based MOFs can be reduced by increasing the size of secondary building units (SBUs) and strengthening the conjugation of organic linkers. Mul and co-workers [49] demonstrated LMCT transition to modulate the band gap by using different dicarboxylate linkers. Zhu et al. [124] fabricated CdS-NPs@MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites through a one-pot solvothermal synthesis, and used it for the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde. The results indicated that the encapsulation of CdS NPs can significantly improve the photocatalytic efficiency as opposed to the pristine CdS semiconductor. The authors concluded that the combination of CdS NPs and MIL-100(Fe) enhances light absorption, changes the band gap energy level, and increases the specific surface area of CdS, thereby enhancing photocatalytic performance.
## 4.2 Promoting charge separation and inhibiting charge recombination
Photogeneration of electrons and holes upon light absorption is the first step of photocatalysis. Both electrons and holes may be used for ROS generation, yet their quick recombination can substantially reduce ROS generation efficiency. A suitable band gap would serve the purpose of efficient electron and hole separation. After that, their efficient transfer to generation ROS could inhibit charge recombination.
In the search for transition metal complexes and organic dyes as luminescent materials and solar cells, donor-acceptor (D-A) type structures were targeted frequently. Upon light absorption, the photogenerated electrons can be transferred from D to A, and holes are left in D. Thus, the electrons and holes can be effectively separated inside the D-A molecules. Compared with individual D or A, D-A molecules usually have narrowed band gaps and redshifted absorption bands. Moreover, D-A molecules may induce the SET process under visible light irradiation and generate radical-ion pairs composed of \(\mathbf{D}^{+}\)and \(\mathbf{A}^{-}\)radicals. For example, our group reported a robust Zn-MOF (JNU-204) with D-A-D type ligands containing two electron-donating pyrazoles and one electron-accepting benzothiadiazole. JNU-204 exhibited significantly improved charge separation efficiency [15]. In addition, a variety of benzothiadiazole organic ligands (non-D-A-D type) were used to construct corresponding MOFs as control. For the phenylboronic acid oxidation reaction, the results show that the construction of D-A-D type organic linkers into MOFs can significantly improve the charge separation efficiency and enhance the catalytic activity of the reaction (Table 1).
2D materials have been proved to be excellent in electron transport and are widely applied in the field of batteries, photovoltaics, and heterogeneous catalysis [125–127]. Researchers have discovered that the catalytic ability of 3D MOFs is largely dependent on the outside surface of the MOF particles instead of their internal cavities [10,57,88]. 2D-MOFs are expected to not only increase the catalytically active surface but also overcome the electron and hole transfer resistance, thereby improving the charge separation
efficiency. Zhou’s group [58] selected a previously reported 2D-MOF, Zr-BTB \((\mathrm{BTB}=1,3,5\)-tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene), as the carrier of the catalyst. 2D layered Zr-BTB nanosheets were first obtained with 6 pairs of terminal \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)groups on the upper and lower layers; then, the terminal \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)groups were substituted with photocatalytically active porphyrin carboxylate molecules, affording PCN-134-2D. Note that the nanosheets with different thicknesses can be achieved by tuning the van der Waals interactions between the layers. Compared with PCN-134-3D, the one directly synthesized from BTB and porphyrin carboxylate, PCN-134-2D was demonstrated improved ROS generation efficiency and charge transfer efficiency, showing the potential of practical application in the photocatalytic synthesis of artemisinin (color online).
In 2020, Huang’s group [59] successfully prepared a series of porphyrin-based 2D lanthanide MOFs (Ln-TCPP, \(\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Ce}\), Sm, Eu, Tb, and Yb; \(\mathrm{TCPP}=\) tetraalkyl(4-carboxyl) porphyrin) with different thicknesses using a household microwave oven (Figure 9). Similarly, the author found that the synthesized Ln-TCPP nanosheets were thickness-dependent for the oxidation of 1,5-dihydroxynaphthalene (1,5-DHN), in which Yb-TCPP showed the best photocatalytic performance. To elucidate the thickness-dependent mechanism of its photocatalytic activity, a photocurrent measurement was carried out and the results showed that the photocurrent density was increased as the thickness of the nanosheets was decreased, confirming that the thinner the film, the higher the charge separation efficiency.
