## 5 Aerobic oxidation reactions photocatalyzed by MOFs
In this section, we summarized the aerobic oxidative coupling reactions photocatalyzed by MOF materials and categorized them according to the types of bonds formed.
## 5.1 Formation of \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) bond
The \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) bond formation reaction is one of the most common processes in organic synthesis, and a key step in the construction of complex molecules from simple precursors. Visible light-driven selective \(\alpha\)-functionalization of tertiary amines enables the construction of a variety of amine derivatives. Among them, the synthesis of \(N\)-aryltetrahydroisoquinolines (THIQ) and its derivatives is of great practical significance because it exists in various natural
compounds and is widely used in aza-Henry reactions [128,129]. As promising heterogeneous photocatalysts, MOFs are frequently used in the organic transformation of THIQ and their derivatives.
cation under visible light irradiation. Then, UNLPF-12 promotes the reduction of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to active species \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\), and the tertiary amine radical cation is deprotonated by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) to form the highly reactive imine cation intermediate and hydroperoxide radical (HOO). Finally, the imine cation is attacked by an enamine nucleophile generated by \(L\)-proline-activated acetone, yielding the desired cross-coupling product (Figure 10). Reusability test and PXRD patterns before and after photocatalysis indicate the good recyclability and stability of UNLPF-12.
Multiphoton absorption is a nonlinear optical phenomenon that occurs when multiple photons are simultaneously absorbed by a material; such a photochemical process can accumulate the energy of visible light, realizing the photocatalysis of C–C coupling reaction which would otherwise require high-energy UV light. In 2018, Duan's group [130] applied a mixed-ligand strategy and constructed a series of two-photon responsive MOFs with bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)-methylpyridinium and bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)pyridine. The obtained ZJU-56-X (X = bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)-methylpyridinium/bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)pyridine = 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6) were used as photocatalysts for the C–C coupling reaction of nitromethane and THIQ. Experimental results show that the increase of bis(3,5-dicarboxyphenyl)-methylpyridinium broadens the visible light-absorption of the MOF and in
troduces more catalytically active sites, which improves the efficiency of the \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) coupling reaction. The porosity of MOFs plays important roles in this work. The photocatalytic reaction was driven by near-infrared light \(\left(660\;\mathrm{nm}\right)\) , which produces the least by-products compared with the ones driven by high-energy UV light. Moreover, the conversion rate of the \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) coupling reaction remained unchanged when the particle size of ZJU-56-0.2 was reduced to \(0.5{-}1~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) , indicating that the reaction mainly occurred in the pores of ZJU-56-0.2 instead of on the outside surface. Importantly, a variety of \(N\!\cdot\) -aryltetrahydroisoquinolines with different substitution patterns and electronic nature were investigated and the desired products were obtained in moderate yields (Table 2). The reaction mechanism is similar to the above-discussed photocatalytic Mannich reaction. After the oxidation, reduction, and deprotonation, the resulting imine cation intermediate reacts with the nucleophilic reagent (nitromethane) to form the target molecule. In addition,
when nitromethane was replaced by cyanotrimethylsilane (TMSCN), dialkyl phosphonates, or dialkylmalonates, the corresponding products can also be obtained through the activation of \(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) bonds [129,131].
## 5.2 Formation of C–O bond
Phenol and its derivatives are crucial raw materials for manufacturing high-value-added chemicals in the field of pharmaceutical and chemical industries. Numerous synthetic methods have been developed to produce phenol and its derivatives, such as the hydroxylation of arylboronic acids. In pursuit of environmentally friendly chemical transformation, MOFs have been developed as heterogeneous photocatalysts for the oxidative hydroxylation of arylboronic acids in the presence of visible light and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) . The excellent stability of \(Z\mathrm{r/Hf}\) -based MOFs has attracted a large number of researchers to use them as matrices to construct heterogeneous photocatalysts. For example, in 2015, Cohen's group [132] synthesized a photocatalyst by incorporating \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) complexes into UiO-67-bpy via a combination of mixed-ligand and PSM strategies and used it for the aerobic oxidative hydroxylation of arylboronic acids under near-UV and visible light irradiation. Matsuoka's group [133] synthesized a porphyrin-based Zr-MOF (MOF-525) and applied it as a photocatalyst for the aerobic oxidative hydroxylation of arylboronic acids with different substituents under green light irradiation in quantitative yield. The control experiment observed no reactions in the absence of MOF-525, green light source, triethylamine \(\mathrm{(Et_{3}N)}\) , or \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , indicating that these parameters are indispensable for this reaction. Moreover, the porphyrin ligand itself was tested as a homogeneous photocatalyst under the same conditions; the results further confirm the superb photocatalytic performance of MOF-525 as heterogeneous catalyst for the oxidative conversion of arylboronic acids to phenols. Mechanistic studies show that the
In 2017, Wang's group [134] utilized a mixed-ligand strategy to incorporate a benzothiadiazole-containing ligand into UiO-68, and the obtained UiO-68-S was used in the oxidative hydroxylation of arylboronic acids under air and blue light irradiation. Moreover, the framework of UiO-68-S remains unchanged after five cycles of catalytic reaction. In addition to directly constructing MOFs with photosensitizers as ligands and introducing photosensitive groups through PSM, researchers are also focusing on enhancing the charge separation of the MOF photocatalysts to improve the efficiency of hydroxylation reaction of arylboronic acids. Recently, our group [15] designed a D-A-D type ligand and the constructed Zn-MOF (JNU-204) achieved efficient catalysis in the oxidative hydroxylation of aryl boronic acids under air and blue light irradiation. Control experiments confirmed that the introduction of D-A-D ligands significantly improved the photocatalytic activity compared with the non-D-A-D ligands. Moreover, JNU-204 exhibited excellent catalytic effects on substrates with different substituents, such as \(-\mathrm{CN}\), \(-\mathrm{Br}\), \(-\mathrm{OMe}\), and \(-\mathrm{CHO}\) groups (Table 3).
## 5.3 Formation of C–N bond
Imines are important intermediates for many pharmaceuticals and fine chemicals. Compared with traditional condensation reactions of amines and carbonyl compounds, visible-light-driven aerobic oxidation of benzylamines to imines has many advantages, such as easy access to raw materials, fewer synthetic steps, energy conservation, and no additional additives required. In 2015, Li's group [135] used \(\mathbf{NH}_{2}\)–\(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{I}\)–\(\mathbf{125}(\mathbf{Ti})\) as a heterogeneous photocatalyst for the
oxidization of benzylamines to imines under visible light irradiation, with a conversion rate of \(73\%\) and a selectivity of \(86\%\). In 2018, Jiang's group [136] employed porphyrin-based MOF (PCN-222) as photocatalyst in the oxidation of various benzylamines in air and under 100-W Xe lamp irradiation. All the benzylamines were converted into their corresponding imines with yields up to \(100\%\) in \(80\;\mathrm{min}\). Recently, Pan et al. [137] used 4-[N,N-bis(4-methylbenzoic acid)amino]benzoic acid and its derivatives to construct three uranium-based MOFs with good chemical stability and broad visible light absorption. Under 300-W Xe lamp irradiation, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) was generated in the presence of these uranium-based MOFs, which triggered the photooxidation of benzylamines with a selectivity of \(99\%\) and a conversion rate of more than \(90\%\). Encouraged by the highly efficient photocatalytic performance and light harvesting property of the Zn-based MOF JNU-204, our group constructed an isostructural Co-based MOF JNU-207 with the same D-A-D ligand. The replacement of Zn to Co further broadens the absorption range of visible light, narrows the band gap, and improves the ROS generation efficiency. The benzylamine oxidation was chosen as the probe reaction, and the results confirm that the introduction of Co atom can indeed further enhance the photocatalytic efficiency of this reaction. Overall, moderate to high yields were obtained for benzylamines with different substituents, illustrating the substrate tolerance of JNU-207 as a heterogeneous photocatalyst (Figure 12) [14]. On the basis of the experiment results and previous reports, a plausible mechanism was proposed. Firstly, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) were generated via SET and ET processes, respectively. Then, the photogenerated hole oxidizes amine into amine radical cation \(\mathbf{C}\), which was further converted into iminium \(\mathbf{D}\) by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) or \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Finally, the produced iminium \(\mathbf{D}\) couples with another amine molecule to form intermediate \(\mathbf{E}\), of which an ammonia molecule is released to afford the imine products (Figure 12).
## 5.4 Formation of \(\mathbf{C}{=}\mathbf{O}\) bond
Carbonyl products, including aldehydes, esters, and ketones, are raw materials for pharmaceutical synthesis. Among them, the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohols to aldehydes is an important transformation reaction in the chemical industry. It is of great significance to study the safe and efficient synthesis of aldehydes at room temperature
Considering the potential of metal NPs and porphyrins for the generation of ROS, as well as the stability of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs, Jiang's group [10] incorporated \(\mathrm{Pt}\) nanoclusters into a porphyrinic MOF PCN-224, the obtained composite material PCN-224(M) was investigated as photocatalyst for the aerobic oxidation of alcohols to aldehydes. Experimental results show that the introduction of \(\mathrm{Pt}\) nanoclusters greatly improves yield and selectivity. In addition, the reaction can be carried out in an aqueous solution and the photocatalyst can be recycled at least 6 times without any obvious loss of
catalytic performance. For benzyl alcohols and heteroaromatic alcohols with different substituents, the substrates can be completely converted to the corresponding aldehydes within \(5\,\mathrm{h}\) . Based on experiment results and relevant literature, the author proposed a plausible mechanism. Firstly, \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is generated via an ET process from PCN-224 and Pt nanocluster to \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) . Then an oxidative addition of the \(\mathrm{O-H}\) bond of alcohol to \(\mathrm{Pt}\) nanocluster generates the \(\mathrm{Pt}\) -alcoholate intermediate, which undergoes a \(\upbeta\) -hydride elimination to afford the aldehyde and \(\mathrm{Pt}\) -hydride complex. Finally, the reaction of the \(\mathrm{Pt}\) -hydride complex with \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) regenerates the \(\mathrm{Pt}\) nanocluster, along with the formation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (Figure 13). In this photocatalytic process, \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) played a key role in removing hydrogen atoms adsorbed on Pt surface.
Recently, Lin's group [98] constructed two UiO-67-type MOFs \((\mathbf{Zr_{6}{-}C u/F e{-}1}\) and \(\mathbf{Zr_{6}{-}C u/F e{-}2})\) by combining the mixed-ligand and PSM strategies. The obtained \(\mathbf{Zr_{6}{-}C u/F e-}\) 1 and \(\mathbf{Zr_{6}{-}C u/F e{-}2}\) were subsequently used as photocatalysts for the oxidation of benzyl alcohols. 4-Methylbenzaldehyde can be achieved in \(98\%\) yield under ambient atmosphere and at room temperature with \(0.5\;\mathrm{mol}\%\) of \(\mathbf{Zr_{6}{-}C u/F e{-}1}\) as the photocatalyst and \(13.9\;\mathrm{W}\) \((350{-}700\ \mathrm{nm})\) solid-state plasma light as the light source. Control experiments showed no reactions in the absence of \(\mathbf{Zr_{6}{-}C u/F e{-}1}\) , light, DMSO, or \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , indicating the indispensability of these reaction parameters. In addition, by adding \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) , \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , and OH scavengers respectively, the authors proved that the OH generated in situ in water is the dominant ROS in this reaction. This work demonstrates the great potential of inexpensive earth-abundant metal-based MOF catalysts for photocatalytic organic
transformation. As shown in Table 4, \(\mathbf{Zr_{6}-Cu/Fe-1}\) can be applied in the photocatalytic oxidation of a wide scope of alcohols, affording the corresponding aldehydes in moderate to good yields.
## 5.5 Formation of \(\mathbf{S}=\mathbf{O}\) bond
Sulfur mustard (bis(2-chloroethyl)sulfide, HD) is a toxic protein foaming agent that can cause skin blisters, respiratory irritation, and even life-threatening. Currently, three pathways have been developed to degrade HD, including dehydrohalogenation, hydrolysis, and oxidation [63]. Particularly, the selective oxidation of sulfide into non-toxic sulfoxide is one of the effective methods for HD degradation. In 2015, Farha’s group [63] used a porphyrin-based MOF (PCN-222/ MOF-545) as photocatalyst for selectively oxidizing CEES into CEESO, and the oxidation half-life was estimated to be \(13\;\mathrm{min}\) . In 2019, Zang and co-workers [138] synthesized a \(\mathrm{Ag}_{12}\) cluster and then used 5,10,15,20-tetra(4-pyridyl)porphyrin (TPyP) to substitution small organic molecules on the \(\mathrm{Ag}_{12}\) cluster to construct a MOF material \((\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}_{12}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{y}\mathbf{P})\) . The efficient and selective oxidation of CEES by \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}_{12}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{y}\mathbf{P}\) was attributed to the strong affinity of \(\mathrm{Ag}_{12}\) clusters for CEES as well as the excellent light-absorption ability of the porphyrin ligand. In addition, \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}_{12}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{y}\mathbf{P}\) can achieve the oxidation of CEES within \(90\;\mathrm{s}\) in an \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) environment, which exceeds the reported efficiency of most MOFs for photocatalytic degradation of CEES. Moreover, the reusability test showed that the \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}_{12}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{y}\mathbf{P}\) collected by filtration can be reused at least four times without showing an obvious loss of catalytic
property.
Although \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is commonly recognized as the ROS for the oxidation of CEES, there are a few reports demonstrating that \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) can also participate in the selective oxidation of CEES to CEESO. In 2021, our group [60] reported a series of Ln-MOFs, named M-TCPP-La, achieved selective oxidation of CEES to CEESO by generating \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) . The oxidation half-life was \(2.5\;\mathrm{min}\) when Fe-TCPPP-La was employed as a photocatalyst under blue light irradiation. Experimental results showed that the efficiency of the reaction significantly decreased when \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) quenching agent (N-tert-butyl- \(\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{a}\) -phenylnitrone, PBN) or \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) quenching agent \(\left(\mathrm{NaN}_{3}\right)\) were added in the Fe-TCPPP-La catalyzed reaction, which proved that both \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) were involved in the oxidation of CEES.
Organic sulfides are also important intermediates in fine chemicals and pharmaceutical production [62]. By using a porphyrin-based Zr-MOF (PCN-222) with an 8-connected Zr cluster, Lang’s group [139] recently successfully introduced 4-carboxy-2,2,6,6-tetramethyl piperidine-1-oxyl (HOOC-TEMPO) by replacing the coordinating hydroxyl groups on the clusters. Experiment results showed that the obtained HOOC-TEMPO@PCN-222 had a good catalytic effect on sulfide oxidation, and the catalytic efficiency was positively correlated with the amount of HOOC-TEMPO introduced. Moreover, the catalytic performance of HOOC-TEMPO@PCN-222 was substantially obviously higher than that of PCN-226 with the same ligand and without accessible hydroxyl sites. The recycling experiment further verified the stability of HOOC-TEMPO@PCN-222, and the photocatalytic activity did not decrease significantly after five cycles under the 660-nm red LEDs irradiation (Figure 14a). Studies on reaction mechanisms showed that \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) is generated via a SET process, while the hole initiates the oxidation of HOOC-TEMPO to HOOC-TEMPO+. The obtained HOOC-TEMPO+ oxidizes sulfide to sulfide radical cation, which is then attacked by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}\) to generate persulfoxide. Finally, the target sulfoxide is obtained from persulfoxide with the help of electrons and protons from the methanol solvent. A wide range of sulfide derivatives with different functional groups were also efficiently oxidized under similar conditions, demonstrating the general applicability of HOOC-TEMPO@PCN-222 as photocatalyst in this reaction (Figure 14b).
## 5.6 Other aerobic reactions
In addition to the above-discussed different types of bond formation reactions, several other aerobic oxidative transformations have also been demonstrated to be driven by MOF-based photocatalysts. In 2019, Zhang and co-workers [19] reported a \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) -encapsulated MOF composite \((\mathbf{Ru}(\mathbf{bpy})_{3}@\mathbf{N}\mathbf{K}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}\mathbf{-}4)\) through a template-directed
synthesis method. The obtained \(\mathbf{Ru}(\mathbf{bpy})_{3}@\mathbf{NKMOF-4}\) was used as heterogeneous photocatalyst for the cyclization of tertiary anilines and maleimides. Recently, Wang et al. [140] introduced a thiadiazolopyridine-conjugated terphenyldicarboxylate linker into UiO-68 using a mixed-ligand strategy. Under blue LEDs irradiation, the obtained UiO-68-TDP (TDP = thiadiazolopyridine) was demonstrated photocatalytic activity in the synthesis of THQ skeleton from
tertiary anilines and \(N\!\cdot\) -phenylmaleimide (Table 5, a). Our research group also achieved the above organic transformation by using a MOF (JNU-204) constructed with a D-A-D type ligand as photocatalyst [15]. In addition, JNU-204 exhibited excellent photocatalytic activity in the synthesis of \(\upalpha\) - amino carbonyl compounds via anoxygenation reaction with \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) under visible light irradiation (Table 5, b) [15]. Most of the photocatalytic aerobic reactions reported so far involve ROS generation and further utilizations, yet there are also some photocatalytic aerobic reactions in which the ground state \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) itself was used as an oxidizing agent. For example, \(\upbeta\) -ketosulfones are common structural backbones of many bioactive molecules; our group demonstrated the coupling of arylsulfone substrates with alkynes in the presence of JNU-204 under a visible light irradiation, in which \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) capture by the ethylenic radical was a key step (Table 5, c). Ponnapalli and co-workers [15,141] reported the similar coupling reactions by employing the Cu-doped ZIF-8 as catalysts.
## 6 Conclusions and perspective
As an emerging class of heterogeneous catalytic materials, MOFs have gained strong momentum due to their tunable structures and multifunctional components. At present, the study of MOFs as photocatalysts is in the stage of exploring catalytic capability and comparing catalytic performance. A complete understanding of the reaction mechanism is still
lacking, resulting in the inability to precisely control the activity, selectivity, and reaction dynamics of the catalytic reactions. In this regard, the photocatalytic aerobic oxidation reactions involving ROS could be valuable for better understanding photocatalysis, considering the available techniques for ROS detection and quantification. In this review, we surveyed and summarized recent progress in MOF-catalyzed light-driven aerobic oxidation reactions from the viewpoints of reaction mechanisms and strategies for enhancing catalytic performance. Along with the discussion, we highlighted two outstanding advantages of MOF-based photocatalysts for aerobic oxidation reactions: structural tunability and the ability to integrate highly efficient catalytic components. However, we should acknowledge that MOF photocatalysts are still far from ideal for real-world applications. Efforts required to overcome the limitations of MOFs as photocatalysts are mainly in the following aspects.
(1) Improving the stability of MOFs. Recyclability is an outstanding advantage of heterogeneous catalytic materials. The stability of MOFs in solvents, acid/base solutions, and under mechanical agitations was the prerequisite for heterogeneous catalysis. However, the introduction of multiple active species, including photoactive and reactive species, may inevitably affect the overall robustness of MOFs. Therefore, more consideration and attempts are needed in further designing and applying MOFs to meet the requirements of practical applications while achieving high catalytic activity.
(2) Expanding the diversity of MOF components. The reticular synthesis of MOFs by judiciously selecting metal nodes and organic ligands is one of the reasons that MOFs are recognized as star materials for heterogeneous catalysis. Through bottom-up synthesis, post-synthesis modification, and host-guest encapsulation, multiple optical responsive functions can be integrated into the MOF catalytic system to achieve the synergistic effect and improve the catalytic efficiency.
(3) Reducing the cost of MOFs. To some extent, the remarkable advances that MOFs have achieved in the field of photocatalysis are based on high costs. In the process of promoting the practical application of MOFs, the cost is also one of the issues that needs to be addressed urgently. Therefore, more efforts should be devoted to developing high-cost performance MOF photocatalyst by utilizing the abundant metals on the earth and the linkers that can be synthesized in a simple and large scale.
(4) Enhancing the charge separation and transfer. Compared with semiconductor and quantum dot materials, the low separation efficiency of photogenerated carriers in MOF catalysts severely limits their practical applications. Taking the advantage of the versatility of MOF materials, researchers can tune the structure, composition, size, and morphology of MOFs to improve charge separation efficiency. At the same time, by making full use of the porous characteristics of MOFs, researchers can construct a variety of composite materials, including semiconductor@MOF or quantum dot@MOF, to facilitate charge transfer and realize the synergetic effects of their respective components.
